{"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "019fbc15-fb6d-78f1-abfa-032eaa173456", "team_id": "contest2026_004_TeamFalcons", "github_login": "Foleaf", "tool": "codex", "seq": 48, "ts": "2026-08-01T06:54:36.140Z", "role": "tool", "tool_name": "<result>", "tool_call_id": "call_00_vyCNXnc51CUtZzETiVxt2577", "input": null, "output": "Chunk ID: e4a00e\nWall time: 0.0843 seconds\nProcess exited with code 0\nOriginal token count: 572\nOutput:\nAdd or update an MCP server\n\nUsage: grok mcp add [OPTIONS] <NAME> [COMMAND_OR_URL] [ARGS]...\n\nArguments:\n  <NAME>\n          Server name\n\n  [COMMAND_OR_URL]\n          Command to launch (stdio) or URL to connect to (http, sse)\n\n  [ARGS]...\n          Arguments passed to the server command. Place them after `--` so flags such as `-y` are passed to the server instead of grok\n\nOptions:\n  -t, --transport <TRANSPORT>\n          Transport type. Defaults to stdio\n\n          Possible values:\n          - stdio: Launch a local process and communicate over stdin/stdout\n          - http:  Connect to a remote server over streamable HTTP\n          - sse:   Connect to a remote server over Server-Sent Events\n\n  -s, --scope <SCOPE>\n          Config to write to: user (~/.grok/config.toml) or project (./.grok/config.toml)\n\n          Possible values:\n          - user:    `~/.grok/config.toml`, available in all your projects\n          - project: `./.grok/config.toml`, shared with everyone working in this directory\n          \n          [default: user]\n\n  -e, --env <KEY=value>\n          Environment variable for the server process (repeatable)\n\n  -H, --header <NAME: VALUE>\n          HTTP header for remote servers (repeatable)\n\n      --debug\n          Enable debug logging\n\n      --debug-file <FILE>\n          Write debug logs to FILE\n\n  -h, --help\n          Print help (see a summary with '-h')\n\n      --leader-socket <PATH>\n          Use a custom leader socket path instead of the default `~/.grok/leader.sock`\n\nExamples:\n  # Add a stdio server (everything after -- is the server command)\n  grok mcp add xcode -- xcrun mcpbridge\n\n  # Add a stdio server with environment variables\n  grok mcp add postgres -e DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost/mydb -- npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-postgres\n\n  # Add a remote HTTP server\n  grok mcp add --transport http sentry https://mcp.sentry.dev/mcp\n\n  # Add a remote server with an authentication header\n  grok mcp add --transport http api https://mcp.example.com/mcp --header \"Authorization: Bearer ***REDACTED***\"\n\n  # Add to the project config (./.grok/config.toml) instead of ~/.grok/config.toml\n  grok mcp add --scope project github -- npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-github\n===\nNo MCP servers configured. Run `grok mcp add --help` to get started.\n", "redacted_count": 1}
{"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "019fbc15-fb6d-78f1-abfa-032eaa173456", "team_id": "contest2026_004_TeamFalcons", "github_login": "Foleaf", "tool": "codex", "seq": 49, "ts": "2026-08-01T06:54:36.163Z", "role": "tool", "tool_name": "<result>", "tool_call_id": "call_01_Yc4fjF4Rxy79jDajYyhZ6597", "input": null, "output": "Chunk ID: 68cdde\nWall time: 0.0000 seconds\nProcess exited with code 2\nOriginal token count: 1839\nOutput:\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:153:- **matcher** (optional): A regular expression that selects which invocations trigger the hook. What it tests depends on the event: the tool name on tool events (`PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse`, `PostToolUseFailure`, `PermissionDenied`), the notification type on `Notification`, the subagent type on `SubagentStart`/`SubagentStop` (e.g. `explore`), the start source on `SessionStart` (`startup`, `resume`, …), the end reason on `SessionEnd`, the compaction trigger on `PreCompact`/`PostCompact` (`manual` or `auto`), and the error type on `StopFailure` (`rate_limit`, `authentication_failed`, `invalid_request`, `server_error`, `max_output_tokens`, or `unknown`). A matcher on `Stop` or `UserPromptSubmit` is ignored with a warning (those events always fire). An empty or omitted matcher matches everything. The matcher tests the real tool name; MCP calls routed through the internal `use_tool` / Cursor `CallMcpTool` dispatchers appear as the qualified `server__tool` name (e.g. `linear__save_issue`), so match on that, not the dispatcher name.\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:268:`StopFailure` is observation-only (use it to log failures or send alerts; output and exit code are ignored). Its input carries `error` (the classified type the matcher tests, in Claude Code's vocabulary: `rate_limit`, `authentication_failed`, `invalid_request`, `server_error`, `max_output_tokens`, or `unknown` for anything the runtime cannot distinguish; capacity errors fold into `rate_limit` and there is no signal for `billing_error`), `errorDetails` (the raw error detail, when available), and `lastAssistantMessage` (the rendered error text shown in the conversation; for this event it is the error string, not assistant output).\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:283:- **StopFailure classes**: the emitted set is Claude Code's vocabulary — `rate_limit`, `authentication_failed`, `invalid_request`, `server_error`, `max_output_tokens`, `unknown`. grok emits a subset: capacity errors (503/529) fold into `rate_limit` as in Claude, and `billing_error` is never emitted (no signal), so a `billing_error` matcher will not fire.\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/06-theming.md:25:Syntax highlighting in minimal mode does **not** switch between light and dark theme files (polarity detection is intentionally avoided). Near-gray tokens inherit the terminal default foreground; chromatic tokens use base ANSI accents (red/green/yellow/blue/magenta/cyan) so read-file output and fenced code stay legible on both light and dark profiles.\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:135:ACP/Messages token (`end_turn`, `max_tokens`, …).\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:145:    \"input_tokens\": 7210,\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:146:    \"cache_read_input_tokens\": 41000,\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:147:    \"cache_creation_input_tokens\": 0,\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:148:    \"output_tokens\": 1893,\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:149:    \"reasoning_tokens\": 412,\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:150:    \"total_tokens\": 50103\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:154:      \"inputTokens\": 7210,\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:155:      \"outputTokens\": 1893,\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:156:      \"cacheReadInputTokens\": 41000,\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:168:- `usage` sums tokens for the prompt, including subagents that finished\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:171:- **Token field policy (headless result / `end` / error spend):**\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:172:  - `usage.input_tokens` and `modelUsage.*.inputTokens` are **uncached only**.\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:173:  - `cache_read_input_tokens` / `cacheReadInputTokens` are cache hits.\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:174:  - `total_tokens` is full input + output (includes both cache buckets):\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:175:    `total_tokens = input_tokens + cache_read_input_tokens + cache_creation_input_tokens + output_tokens`.\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:176:  - ACP `_meta.usage.inputTokens` (PromptUsage) is still the **full** prompt\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:198:  (token totals may under-count subagents). Cancel snapshots without that long\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:200:  no recorded tokens emits only `usage_is_incomplete` (no zero `usage` object).\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:221:{\"type\":\"usage\",\"messageId\":\"resp_1\",\"stopReason\":\"end_turn\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":812,\"output_tokens\":45,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"reasoning_tokens\":0},\"signature\":\"...\"}\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:230:| `thought`          | Internal reasoning (thinking tokens)                                                          |\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:240:shape (snake_case uncached `input_tokens`, safe cost floats). `end.stopReason`\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:241:is the turn stop reason in snake_case (`end_turn`, `max_tokens`,\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:261:{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"duration_ms\":0,\"duration_api_ms\":0,\"num_turns\":7,\"result\":\"Here's a summary...\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"total_cost_usd\":0.0127,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":812,\"output_tokens\":210,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0}},\"modelUsage\":{},\"session_id\":\"abc123\",\"uuid\":\"...\"}\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:297:`result.usage` reports the Messages `message.usage` shape with the three token buckets disjoint: `input_tokens` (uncached), `cache_read_input_tokens`, and `cache_creation_input_tokens`. Grok derives these from the turn's aggregate ledger, reshaped into those buckets. Subagent cache creation is included in `cache_creation_input_tokens`. The aggregate ledger tracks it as its own bucket, so it is no longer folded into `input_tokens`.\n/home/debian19y/.grok/docs/user-guide/14-headless-mode.md:307:`--include-partial-messages` emits the raw event framing so a consumer can rebuild each message with the Messages streaming accumulator. The framing is `message_start`, `content_block_start`/`content_block_delta`/`content_block_stop`, `message_delta`, and `message_stop`. It carries the structural events an accumulator needs. The deltas are coarser than the Messages API's token-level streaming: tool input arrives as a single `input_json_delta`, and `citations_delta` is never produced (see below). The result is a faithful reconstruction of each message rather than a token-by-token replay.\n===\n/home/debian19y/.bashrc:140:if [ -z \"${GITHUB_PAT_TOKEN:-}\" ] && command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n/home/debian19y/.bashrc:141:  GITHUB_PAT_TOKEN=\"$(gh auth token 2>/dev/null)\" && export GITHUB_PAT_TOKEN\n"}
